[P3] Describe the direct row pointer used by hardware staging

The changed callback in `h/tron/scheduler/full.hpp` returns `k_row_if_row_major` directly for a row-major block. It calls `get_k_row` only for a converted block. The phrase “a 256-byte copy from a row-major block” therefore describes work that the optimized callback avoids.

Suggested replacement for this consumer description:

```cpp
//   4. The FPGA (field-programmable gate array) staging path
//      (gof::populate -> shuffle_k_entry) needs one row per token.
//      For a row-major block, page_info::k_head_fn returns the row pointer
//      from page::k_row_if_row_major. For a converted block, it gathers
//      the row into caller-owned scratch with page::get_k_row.
//      v_head_fn copies the value row into caller-owned scratch.
//      The staging bytes and the HBM (High Bandwidth Memory,
//      the FPGA device memory) layout are unchanged.
```

[P3] Name the caller's responsibility for publishing layout bits

The phrase “sets the 16 row bits and the block bit itself” attributes publication to `page::set_k_block`, the per-head block writer. That function only writes the values. `store_k_block`, the model-level writer, calls `note_k_block_saved` after storing every key/value head.

That order matters: readers must not observe the converted-block bit before all heads have the corresponding layout.

Suggested replacement:

```cpp
//   - A full-block save (page::set_k_block) writes the VNNI layout directly.
//     After storing every KV head, the caller uses note_k_block_saved to
//     publish the 16 row bits and the block bit. Any thread of a shared
//     window may perform this save. Every row belongs to that one run.
```

Agreed, and changed in a9923ddd97. `set_k_block` stores the 16 rows of one KV (key/value) head and sets no bit. `note_k_block_saved` sets the 16 row bits and the block bit. `store_k_block` calls it once, after its loop over every KV head. The block bit is per slot, shared by every KV head. So that order is the publication contract. The bullet now:

- says `set_k_block` sets no bit;
- names `store_k_block` as the caller that publishes after the last KV head;
- states why the order matters;
- points at Note [Shared block save] for the "any thread of a shared window" part.

The bullet keeps the lowercase start of its sibling bullets.

[P3] Distinguish preserved key values from identical generated tokens

“The tokens are the same either way” can be read as a guarantee of identical generated output. Selecting the correct layout preserves the stored keys. It does not establish that output guarantee.

`k_vnni::qk_group` (the vectorized score reader) documents a different floating-point addition order from the row-major dotter. Changing which reader runs can therefore change scores. This is a documentation concern, not evidence of an observed output failure.

Suggested replacement, with one claim per sentence:

```cpp
// Every reader selects its access pattern from the block bit.
// Both layouts preserve the stored key values.
// The score accumulation order can differ between readers.
```

Agreed, and changed in a9923ddd97. Both layouts hold the same K values. The row-major dotter and `k_vnni::qk_group` add the fp32 (32-bit float) products in a different order (the numerics contract in Note [AMX attention dispatch]). A score can differ by that alone, and so can a generated token. The passage now states three separate claims:

- every reader takes its layout from the block bit, and no stored token is missed;
- both layouts hold the same K values;
- the add order differs, and it can change a score and a token.

Measurements (runtron, 1 user, temperature 0, prompt lengths 1024 and 1000 tokens). On 2026-09-30 the policy build (this PR's head) and the previous fix build produced identical tokens in all four cells (CPU attention and FPGA attention, both prompt lengths). On 2026-09-29, under CPU attention, the fix build differed from the PR #4424 head: first difference at generated token 52 (prompt 1024, 65 of 128 tokens differ) and at token 6 (prompt 1000, 114 of 128 differ). We attribute that to the add order. It is not proven: a forced run of both binaries, comparing the top-k logits at the first divergent step, would settle it.
